-- dispatch: what an `err` part means, and where the only ones a clean
-- program produces come from.
--
-- **A PIN, not a regression fixture.** Nothing was wrong with what this
-- golden records. It is here because the meaning of `err` is an invariant
-- two phases lean
-- on from opposite sides — the checker writes it, `Lower.structuralEq`
-- reads it — and neither side states it anywhere else. A fixture that
-- fails first would be a fixture for a bug; this one exists so that the
-- next change to either side has to say, in a golden diff, that it meant
-- to move the line.
--
-- `err` is the target the checker writes when it cannot NAME a function for
-- a position, and the backend leans on it: `Lower.structuralEq` answers
-- `true` for an `err` part, so a comparison whose other parts are
-- structural still goes through `core/Basics.js`'s `eq`, and a derived body
-- emits `Basics$eq` at that position. That is only safe while `err` means
-- one thing, so this golden is where the one thing is written down.
--
-- (2026-10-02: `err` is now the `undetermined` leaf, and the leaf and the
-- evidence for an undetermined element are `===` — `primitive strict_eq` —
-- and no longer `Basics.eq`, which dispatches like `==` since that day,
-- `static-dispatch-spike.md` §3.1. The point stands: the answer is right
-- only because nothing, or only a number, ever reaches the position.)
--
-- It is a position the comparison NEVER REACHES: a type variable nothing
-- constrains. `[] == []` compares two empty lists, so the element type is
-- free, and `Nothing == Nothing` carries no payload.
--
-- `crossed` used to be a second way, and is not any more
-- (static-dispatch-spike.md A.86): `Ok 1 == Err "a"` is a `Result String
-- Int`, but the RECEIVER (`Ok 1`) pins only `a` when the constraint is
-- discharged, and `x` arrives from the argument afterwards. The part is
-- now a PART SITE, answered when `x` is: `strict_eq`, not `err`. The same
-- lateness in a `let` wrote `err` for a position that did become a user's
-- type with its own `eq`, and the backend's structural answer was wrong.
--
-- What `err` is NOT, and the reason the last three declarations are here:
-- an unresolved `number`. A.59 makes `targetFor` ask the builtin bridge
-- before it gives up, so a `number` element of a derived shape answers
-- `strict_eq` (for `eq`) or `num_compare` (for `compare`) — the same
-- answers §3.2 gives at the top level — and never `err`. Before that every
-- literal position of a derived shape was a hole.
--
-- The moment the checker starts writing `err` for a position it could not
-- resolve for some OTHER reason — a user's own `pub eq` it failed to find,
-- say — the backend silently routes that position through the structural
-- walk and answers the wrong `Bool`. No `run/` fixture can see the
-- difference, because both readings compile and only one is right. This
-- golden can.
pub empties : Bool
empties = [] == []


pub nothings : Bool
nothings = Nothing == Nothing


pub crossed : Bool
crossed = Ok 1 == Err "a"


pub insideAShape : Bool
insideAShape = { a = [] } == { a = [] }


pub literalPair : Bool
literalPair = ( 1, 2.5 ) == ( 3, 4 )


pub literalOrder : Bool
literalOrder = ( 1, 2 ) < ( 3, 4 )
